<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Erzurum</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Theodosiopolis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Erzurum is a city on a high plateau in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, near the sources of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>. Known in late antiquity as Theodosiopolis, it guarded the routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> and was ruled in turn by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>, the Saltukids and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>. The Congress of Erzurum of 1919 was an early step in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkish-war-of-independence">Turkish War of Independence</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/erzurum.jpg" alt="Erzurum" width="320" height="166" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zeynel Cebeci · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Erzurum_Citadel_-_Erzurum_Kalesi_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
